  "feedback": "# Agent 3 \u2192 Agent 1: Top Performer Analysis & Generation Instructions\n\n## Performance Analysis\n\nExcellent work! The top 3 curves have converged on a highly successful design pattern with scores of **0.942, 0.940, and 0.940**. All three achieve:\n- **Smoothness: 0.924-0.928** (excellent trajectory continuity)\n- **Time Uniformity: 0.865-0.871** (good temporal distribution)\n- **Sample Density: 0.990-0.992** (near-perfect spatial coverage)\n\n## Key Success Patterns Identified\n\n### 1. **High Line Density Standard**\nAll top curves use **num_lines = 300**, which has proven optimal for balancing coverage and smoothness.\n\n### 2. **Multi-Frequency Perturbation Strategy**\nThe winners employ 4-5 independent sinusoidal perturbations:\n- **X-axis**: 2 perturbations with prime frequencies (17-19 primary, 31-37 secondary)\n- **Y-axis**: 2 perturbations with prime frequencies (23 primary, 29-41 secondary)\n- **Amplitudes**: Primary 0.007-0.012, Secondary 0.003-0.004\n\n### 3. **Prime Number Frequencies**\nAll successful curves use **prime-number frequencies** (13, 17, 19, 23, 29, 31, 37, 41) to avoid resonance patterns and ensure aperiodic perturbations.\n\n### 4. **Amplitude Hierarchy**\nLarger amplitudes (0.007-0.012) on primary frequencies, smaller (0.003-0.004) on secondary frequencies, maintaining smoothness while adding complexity.\n\n### 5. **Optional Coupling Term**\ncurve_5_v7.py includes a coupled perturbation: `coupled_amp * sin(2\u03c0 * 13 * (x + y))` with amplitude 0.004, which may enhance coverage without sacrificing smoothness.\n\n## Generation Instructions for Agent 1\n\n**Generate exactly 5 new curve variations** exploring these specific modifications:\n\n### Variation 1: Extended Multi-Frequency System\n- Base: curve_5_v7.py architecture\n- Add a **6th and 7th frequency**: Include `x_amp_3 = 0.002`, `x_freq_3 = 43`, `y_amp_3 = 0.002`, `y_freq_3 = 47`\n- Test if ultra-high frequency diversity further improves density\n\n### Variation 2: Optimized Coupling Exploration\n- Base: curve_2_v7.py architecture\n- Add coupling term: `coupling_amp = 0.005`, `coupling_freq = 11`\n- Apply: `x += coupling_amp * sin(2\u03c0 * coupling_freq * (x + y))`\n- Explore if coupling improves time uniformity score\n\n### Variation 3: Amplitude Gradient Variation\n- Base: curve_5_v7.py\n- Modify amplitudes to: `x_amp_1 = 0.010`, `x_amp_2 = 0.005`, `y_amp_1 = 0.008`, `y_amp_2 = 0.004`, `coupled_amp = 0.003`\n- Test slightly larger, evenly graduated amplitudes\n\n### Variation 4: Ultra-Prime Frequency Set\n- Base: curve_2_v7.py\n- Replace with higher primes: `x_primary_freq = 53`, `x_secondary_freq = 59`, `y_primary_freq = 61`, `y_secondary_freq = 67`\n- Keep amplitudes identical to original\n- Test if higher primes reduce any remaining periodic artifacts\n\n### Variation 5: Hybrid Coupling System\n- Base: curve_5_v7.py\n- Add second coupling: `coupling_amp_2 = 0.003`, `coupling_freq_2 = 19`\n- Apply both: `x += coupling_amp * sin(2\u03c0 * 13 * (x+y))` and `y += coupling_amp_2 * sin(2\u03c0 * 19 * (x-y))`\n- Explore asymmetric coupling effects"
